# size 20x5
# The alternate screen, in and out. Nothing else in the corpus leaves it: the vim and less captures
# were killed while still on it, so they test entering and never restoring - which is the half that
# makes "your shell comes back when you quit vim" work.
#
# Write on the primary screen, switch (which saves the cursor and clears), write something else,
# switch back. The primary text and the cursor must both be where they were, and nothing written on
# the alternate screen may survive.
\e[H\e[2Jkept-one\r\nkept-two\r\n\e[2;4H
\e[?1049h
OVERWRITTEN\r\nalso-gone
\e[?1049l
